pillbox+recWG+load.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: ./pillbox+recWG+load.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition : {
    Magnetic:  1, 2
    Exterior:  6
  }
  Material : {
    Attribute: 1
    Epsilon:   1.0
    Mu:        1.0
  }
  Material : {
    Attribute: 2
    Epsilon:   10.0
    EpsilonImag: -1.52         // loss tangent 0.152
    Mu:        1.0
  }
  SurfaceMaterial : {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order:           1
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

EigenSolver : {
  NumEigenvalues: 2
  FrequencyShift:  1.1e9
}
</code></pre> <p><code>EpsilonImag</code> is negative for a lossy material. There is no <code>Waveguide</code> boundary and no <code>Port</code> block; the loss comes entirely from the volume. The problem is still a complex eigenproblem.</p>

dlwg-pbc4-m0.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: ./pbc-4.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition : {
    Exterior: 6
    Magnetic: 1, 2
    Periodic_M: 3              // master face
    Periodic_S: 4              // slave face
    Theta: -150                // phase advance per cell, degrees
  }
  SurfaceMaterial: {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order:           2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

EigenSolver : {
  NumEigenvalues: 1
  FrequencyShift:  1.10e9
}
</code></pre> <p><code>Periodic_M</code> and <code>Periodic_S</code> name the master and slave sidesets. <code>Theta</code> is the phase advance in degrees. The sign convention follows the direction from master to slave. For a \(2\pi/3\) structure, set <code>Theta</code> to 120 or -120 and compare the frequency against the design.</p>

pillbox-rtop.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: ./pillbox-rtop4.ncdf

  BoundaryCondition : {
    Magnetic: 1, 2          // symmetry planes: monopole modes
    Exterior: 6             // cavity wall
  }

  SurfaceMaterial : {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7            // copper, S/m, for Q0
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order:           2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

EigenSolver : {
  NumEigenvalues: 2
  FrequencyShift:  1.0e9    // look for modes above 1 GHz
}

PostProcess : {
  Toggle: on                // write field files for ParaView
  ModeFile: mode
}
</code></pre> <p>Two magnetic symmetry planes select modes whose electric field is normal to both planes, which for a cylindrical cavity means the monopole family. To get dipole modes, change one plane to <code>Electric</code>.</p> <p>The default volume material is vacuum, so no <code>Material</code> block is needed. If you want to be explicit:</p> <pre><code>  Material : {
    Attribute: 1            // Cubit block ID
    Epsilon: 1.0
    Mu: 1.0
  }
</code></pre>

pillbox-rtop+coax.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: ./pillbox-rtop+coax2.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition : {
    Magnetic:  2
    Electric:  3, 4          // beam pipe ends, below cutoff
    Exterior:  6
    Waveguide: 7             // coax port
  }
  SurfaceMaterial : {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order:           2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
  Tolerance: 1e-7
}

EigenSolver : {
  NumEigenvalues: 1
  FrequencyShift:  1.0e9
}

Port : {
  ReferenceNumber : 7
  NumberOfModes : 1          // the coax TEM mode
}

PostProcess : {
  Toggle: on
  ModeFile: mode
}
</code></pre> <p>The beam pipe ends are closed with <code>Electric</code>. That is fine here because the pipe is below cutoff at 1.3 GHz, so the field there has decayed to nothing. The coax port is <code>Waveguide</code> with one mode loaded: a coax has a TEM mode with zero cutoff, and nothing else propagates at this frequency.</p>

LCLSGun.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: LCLSGun-DriveField.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition : {
    Magnetic: 1 2        // symmetry planes and small ports
    Magnetic: 3          // beam pipe
    Exterior: 4 6        // cathode and cavity walls
    Magnetic: 5          // waveguide
  }
}
FiniteElement: { Order: 1  CurvedSurfaces: on }
EigenSolver : { FrequencyShift: 2.80e9  NumEigenvalues: 2 }
</code></pre> <p>The two modes of a 1.6-cell gun are the 0 mode and the π mode. Omega3P finds them at 2.83750 GHz (Q<sub>0</sub> 13,112) and 2.85281 GHz (Q<sub>0</sub> 13,925). The π mode, in which the two cells are 180 degrees apart, is the accelerating mode and is mode 1 in Omega3P's zero-based count.</p> <p></p> <p>E<sub>z</sub> of the π mode on the symmetry plane. The field reverses between the half cell at the cathode and the full cell.</p>

90DegreeBend.s3p<pre><code>ModelInfo: {
  File: ./90DegreeBend.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition: {
    Exterior: 6
    Waveguide: 7, 8
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order: 2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

FrequencyScan: {
  Start:    9.424e+9
  End:      12.424e+9
  Interval: 0.25e+9
}

PostProcess: {
  Toggle: on
  PortNumber: 7
}

Port: {
  ReferenceNumber: 7
  NumberOfModes: 2
}
Port: {
  ReferenceNumber: 8
  NumberOfModes: 2
}
</code></pre> <p>No <code>SurfaceMaterial</code> block is given, so the walls are treated as lossless. Two modes are loaded per port. Only TE<sub>10</sub> propagates below 13.1 GHz, so the second mode is evanescent across the scan and is included to absorb near-port field distortion.</p>

recWG-load1.s3p<pre><code>ModelInfo: {
  File: ./recWG-load1.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition: {
    Magnetic: 1
    Exterior: 6
    Waveguide: 7
  }
  Material : {
    Attribute: 1
    Epsilon:   1.0
    Mu:        1.0
  }
  Material : {
    Attribute: 2
    Epsilon:   10.0
    Mu:        1.0
    EpsilonImag: -1.52
  }
  SurfaceMaterial: {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order: 2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

FrequencyScan: {
  Start:    2.556e+9
  End:      3.156e+9
  Interval: 0.05e+9
}

PostProcess: {
  Toggle: on
  PortNumber: 7
}

Port: {
  ReferenceNumber: 7
  NumberOfModes: 2
}
</code></pre> <p>The material blocks are the same as in the Omega3P lossy load tutorial. Block attribute 2 is the wedge, with a negative imaginary permittivity for loss.</p>

window.s3p<pre><code>ModelInfo: {
  File: ./window.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition: {
    Magnetic: 1
    Electric: 2
    Exterior: 6
    Waveguide: 7, 8
  }
  Material : {
    Attribute: 1
    Epsilon:   1.0
    Mu:        1.0
  }
  Material : {
    Attribute: 2
    Epsilon:   9.37
    Mu:        1.0
  }
  SurfaceMaterial: {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order: 2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

FrequencyScan: {
  Start:    2.356e9
  End:      3.856e9
  Interval: 0.1e+9
}
</code></pre> <p>The quarter model uses one magnetic and one electric symmetry plane, which is the correct pair for the TE<sub>10</sub> mode of the rectangular guide. Without <code>Port</code> blocks, S3P loads one mode per waveguide surface, which is right for a single-moded guide.</p>

pillboxwg-closed.t3p<pre><code>ModelInfo: {
  File: ./pillboxwg.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition: {
    Exterior: 6 5             // wall and closed waveguide end
    Absorbing: 3 4            // beam pipe ends
    Magnetic: 1 2             // symmetry planes
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order: 2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

LoadingInfo: {
  Bunch: {
    Type: Gaussian
    Sigma: 0.01
    Number of sigmas: 5
    Charge: 1.e-12
  }
  SymmetryFactor: 4
  StartPoint: 0.0, 0.0, -0.075
  Direction: 0.0, 0.0, 1.0
  BoundaryID: 3
}

TimeStepping: {
  MaximumTime: 5e-9
  DT: 2e-12
}

Monitor: {
  Type: Volume
  Name: mymon
  TimeStart: 0e-9
  TimeEnd:   5e-9
  TimeStep:  0.2e-9
}

Monitor: {
  Type: WakeField
  Name: wakefield
  StartContour: -0.075
  EndContour:    0.075
  Smax: 1.4
}

LinearSolver: {
  Solver: MUMPS
}
</code></pre> <p>Two magnetic symmetry planes with a beam on the axis select the monopole wake. The bunch enters through surface 3, which must be an absorbing boundary so that the beam's own field is not reflected. The wake contour spans the whole model from pipe end to pipe end. <code>Smax</code> of 1.4 m records the wake for about 4.7 ns behind the bunch, which matches <code>MaximumTime</code>.</p>

BPM.t3p<pre><code>ModelInfo: {
  File: BPM.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition: {
    Electric: 6
    Absorbing: 1 2 5          // pipe ends and coax end
    Magnetic: 3 4
  }
  Material : { Attribute: 1  Epsilon: 1.0  Mu: 1.0 }
  Material : { Attribute: 2  Epsilon: 4.9  Mu: 1.0 }    // ceramic
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order: 2
  Curved Surfaces: on
}

LoadingInfo: {
  Bunch: {
    Type: Gaussian
    Sigma: 0.005
    Nsigmas: 5
    Charge: 1.e-12
  }
  SymmetryFactor: 4
  StartPoint: 0.0 0.0 -0.025
  Direction: 0.0, 0.0, 1.0
  BoundaryID: 1
}

TimeStepping: {
  MaximumTime: 2.0e-9
  DT: 0.5e-12
}

Monitor: {
  Type: WakeField
  Name: wakefield
  Start contour: -0.0055
  End contour:    0.0055
  Smax: 0.35
}

Monitor: { Type: Point  Name: point      Coordinate: 0 0 0 }
Monitor: { Type: Point  Name: coaxpoint  Coordinate: 0.01457 0.03586 -0.0020955 }

Monitor: {
  Type: Volume
  Name: volume
  TimeStart: 0.000e-9
  TimeEnd:   0.4e-9
  TimeStep:  0.02e-9
}

// These two monitors feed acdtool postprocess coaxsignal.
// The names "port" and "modecoeff" are required.
Monitor: {
  Type: Power
  ReferenceNumber: 5
  Name: port
}

Monitor: {
  Type: ModeVoltage
  Name: modecoeff
  Port: {
    ReferenceNumber: 5
    ESolver: {
      Type: Numerical2D
      NumberOfModes: 1
      Frequency: 1.e9
      Tolerance: 1.e-15
      MaxIterations: 1000
    }
  }
}

LinearSolver: {
  Slover: CG
}
</code></pre> <p>Several things to notice:</p> <ul> <li>The wall is <code>Electric</code>, not <code>Exterior</code>: wall loss is irrelevant over 2 ns, and the perfect conductor is cheaper.</li> <li>The 5 mm bunch has content to about 10 GHz, so <code>DT</code> is 0.5 ps, four times finer than the pillbox examples.</li> <li><code>Curved Surfaces: on</code> with a space, <code>Nsigmas</code>, and <code>Start contour</code> with a space are all accepted alternate spellings.</li> <li>The coax end, surface 5, is <code>Absorbing</code> and carries both a <code>Power</code> and a <code>ModeVoltage</code> monitor. The <code>ESolver</code> sub-block tells T3P to compute the coax TEM mode numerically on that surface so the modal amplitude can be projected out at each step.</li> <li>The <code>LinearSolver</code> block has <code>Slover: CG</code>, misspelled. The parser ignores unknown keys, so <code>Solver</code> is absent and the default applies, which in a build with MUMPS is MUMPS. The stored results were therefore produced with the direct solver, not CG as the author intended.</li> </ul>

SIBC.t3p<pre><code>CheckPoint: {
  Action: restart
  Ntimesteps: 500
}

ModelInfo: {
  File: test-sibc.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition: {
    Electric: 6
    Magnetic: 1 2
    Impedance: 3              // the coated wire section
    Absorbing: 4 5            // ports
  }
  SurfaceMaterial: {
    ReferenceNumber: 3
    Sigma: 5.8e7              // copper beneath
    Frequency: 5e9            // reference for the narrow-band model
    Coating: {
      Type: ThinLossyDielectric
      Epsilon:   56
      Sigma: 12               // S/m
      Thickness: 1.e-3        // m
    }
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order: 0
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

PRegion: {
  Type: AutomaticMovingWindow
  Object: Pulse
  StartPoint: 0.01 0.05 -0.5
  Direction: 0 0 1
  ExcitationPort: 4
  Order: 2
  Back:  0.1
  Front: 0.1
  StructureEnd: 0.5
}

Loading: {
  Type: PortModeLoading
  ScalingFactor: 1.0
  Port: {
    ReferenceNumber: 4
    SymmetricFactor: 4
    NumberOfModes: 1          // TEM
  }
  Excitation: {
    Power: 1.
    Pulse: {
      Type: Gaussian
      Frequency: 5e9          // carrier
      Bandwidth: 2e9
      DB: 30                  // truncate the envelope 30 dB down
      T0: 2.82                // delay in sigmas
      Phase: 0
    }
  }
}

TimeStepping: {
  MaximumTime: 1.0e-8
  DT: 10.e-12
}

Monitor: { Type: Volume  Name: field  TimeStart: 0  TimeEnd: 1.0e-8  TimeStep: 0.5e-9 }
Monitor: { Type: Power   ReferenceNumber: 4  Name: inputPower }
Monitor: { Type: Power   ReferenceNumber: 5  Name: outputPower }
Monitor: { Type: Power   ReferenceNumber: 3  Name: wallossPower }

LinearSolver: {
  Solver: CG
}
</code></pre> <p>The excitation is a Gaussian-enveloped 5 GHz pulse of 2 GHz bandwidth launched as the TEM mode from port 4. The moving window follows it with <code>Object: Pulse</code>; <code>Back</code> and <code>Front</code> of 0.1 m bracket the roughly 0.3 m long pulse. <code>CheckPoint.Action: restart</code> here means the run will resume from a checkpoint if one exists.</p> <p>Key spellings differ between blocks</p> <p>The port block inside <code>Loading</code> uses <code>SymmetricFactor</code>, while <code>LoadingInfo</code> for beams uses <code>SymmetryFactor</code>. Both are as the code reads them.</p>

RfGunVacuum.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo: {
  File: RfGunVacuum.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition: {
    Electric: 1
    Magnetic: 2 3 4
    Electric: 5
    Exterior: 6
  }
}
FiniteElement: { Order: 2  Curved Surfaces: on }
EigenSolver: { NumEigenvalues: 1  FrequencyShift: 2.8e9 }
</code></pre> <p>The waveguide port, sideset 5, is closed with an electric wall for this eigenmode calculation. Result: 2.8533884 GHz, Q<sub>0</sub> 15,080.</p>

FPC-Body.tem3p (abridged)<pre><code>ThermostaticProblem: {
  MeshFile: 2D-TTF3-FPC-Body.ncdf
  BasisOrder: 1
  CurvedSurfaces: on

  NonlinearSolver: {
    Solver: Newton
    PicardIteration: 100
    MaxIterations: 500
  }
  PicardSolver: { Solver: CG  Preconditioner: DIAGONAL  Tolerance: 1.0e-10  MaxIterations: 100000 }
  LinearSolver: { Solver: GMRES  Preconditioner: ILU  Tolerance: 1.0e-10  MaxIterations: 100000 }

  // copper plating as shells
  Shell: { Bd: 6  Material: 221  BasisOrder: 1  Thickness: 1e-5 }
  Shell: { Bd: 7  Material: 221  BasisOrder: 1  Thickness: 1e-4 }
  ThermalConductivity: { Id: 221  Function: functions/CURRR100 }   // RRR 100 copper

  // bulk materials, all temperature dependent
  ThermalConductivity: { Id: 1  Function: functions/HighRRRNb }
  ThermalConductivity: { Id: 2  Function: functions/NbTi }
  ThermalConductivity: { Id: 3  Function: functions/ss304 }
  ThermalConductivity: { Id: 4  Function: functions/CURRR100 }
  ThermalConductivity: { Id: 5  Function: functions/al300 }

  // dielectric loss in the window volume
  HeatSource: {
    Id: 5
    ConditionType: RFHeating
    WhichMode: 0
    DielectricConstantE: 9
    LossTangentE: 0.0001
    Directory: s3p_results
    Method: Powerinput
    TargetPowerinput: 437.5             // 7 kW / 16 for the symmetric sector
  }

  // intercepts
  Boundary: { Id: 2  ConditionType: Dirichlet  DirichletValue: 2 }
  Boundary: { Id: 3  ConditionType: Dirichlet  DirichletValue: 4 }
  Boundary: { Id: 4  ConditionType: Dirichlet  DirichletValue: 70 }
  Boundary: { Id: 5  ConditionType: Dirichlet  DirichletValue: 300 }

  // RF heating on plated and bare surfaces
  Boundary: { Id: 6  ConditionType: RFHeating  WhichMode: 0  Directory: s3p_results
              Method: Powerinput  TargetPowerinput: 437.5  SurfaceResistance: functions/SRinnerCU }
  Boundary: { Id: 7  ... same as 6 ... }
  Boundary: { Id: 8  ... same as 6 ... }
  Boundary: { Id: 9  ConditionType: RFHeating  WhichMode: 0  Directory: s3p_results
              Method: Powerinput  TargetPowerinput: 437.5  SurfaceResistance: functions/SRinnerNbTi }

  Boundary: { Id: 1   ConditionType: Neumann  NeumannValue: 0.0 }   // rest
  Boundary: { Id: 10  ConditionType: Neumann  NeumannValue: 0.0 }   // symmetry
  Boundary: { Id: 11  ConditionType: Neumann  NeumannValue: 0.0 }   // symmetry
}
</code></pre> <p>Four features appear here for the first time:</p> <ul> <li>Nonlinear solve. With any <code>Function</code> material, TEM3P iterates: <code>PicardIteration</code> fixed-point steps using the <code>PicardSolver</code>, then Newton steps using the <code>LinearSolver</code>. GMRES with ILU is chosen for the Newton system, which is not symmetric.</li> <li>Shells. Two <code>Shell</code> blocks add the copper plating to sidesets 6 and 7 as conducting layers of 10 and 100 μm, referencing a <code>ThermalConductivity</code> with the arbitrary Id 221.</li> <li>Temperature-dependent surface resistance. <code>SurfaceResistance: functions/SRinnerCU</code> replaces <code>Sigma</code>, so the RF loss on each surface follows the local temperature through the resistivity curve. This is the correct coupling for a part that spans 2 K to 300 K.</li> <li>Volumetric dielectric heating. <code>HeatSource</code> with <code>DielectricConstantE</code> and <code>LossTangentE</code> deposits the dielectric loss from the S3P field in the window volume.</li> </ul> <p></p> <p>The function files are piecewise expressions in <code>T</code>:</p> functions/ss304<pre><code>-10000 2.1   0.1124
2.1    300   10^((-1.4087)+(1.3982)*log10(T)+(0.2543)*(log10(T))^2+ ... )
300    10000 15.30865
</code></pre>

SRFCell.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo: {
  File: SRFCellVacuum.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition: {
    Magnetic: 1          // symmetry plane for the TM0 mode
    Magnetic: 2 3        // disk centers for the pi mode
    Electric: 6          // Nb wall, lossless
  }
}
FiniteElement: { Order: 2  Curved Surfaces: on }
EigenSolver: { NumEigenvalues: 1  FrequencyShift: 1.2e9 }
</code></pre> <p>Magnetic walls at the cell boundaries select the π mode of the periodic structure. The wall is a perfect conductor since superconducting loss is negligible for this purpose, so Omega3P reports Q<sub>0</sub> as infinite. Frequency: 1.3009577 GHz.</p>

Pillbox.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: ./Pillbox.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition : {
    Exterior: 1, 2, 6
  }
  SurfaceMaterial : {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}
FiniteElement: {
  Order:           2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}
EigenSolver : {
  NumEigenvalues: 2
  FrequencyShift:  1.0e9
}
</code></pre> <p>Sidesets 1 and 2 are the beam pipe ends and 6 is the wall. All three are <code>Exterior</code> here because this is a full model with no symmetry planes. Omega3P finds TM<sub>010</sub> at 1.31381 GHz and, because nothing suppresses dipoles in a full model, the first dipole mode at 1.74993 GHz as mode 2. Track3P loads mode 0 by default (<code>Domain.ModeID1</code>), so the dipole mode is computed but unused.</p> <p>Mode file is required</p> <p>Track3P reads the <code>.mod</code> field file, so the <code>PostProcess</code> block in the Omega3P input must be on, or omitted so that the default applies. If Omega3P was run with <code>PostProcess: { Toggle: off }</code>, rerun it.</p>

Pillbox.track3p<pre><code>FieldScales: {
  Type: FieldGradient
  ScanToken: 1
  Minimum: 23.0e+6
  Maximum: 25.0e+6
  Interval: 1.0e+6
}

NormalizedField: {
  StartPoint: 0.0 0.0 -0.057
  EndPoint:   0.0 0.0  0.057
}

Domain: {
  FieldDir: ./omega3p_results
}

Emitter: {
  BoundaryID: 6
  x0: 0.03   x1: 0.035
  y0: 0.04   y1: 0.1
  z0: -0.045 z1: 0.0
}

Material: { Type: Primary    BoundarySurfaceID: 6 }
Material: { Type: Secondary  BoundarySurfaceID: 6 }
Material: { Type: Absorber   BoundarySurfaceID: 1 2 }

OutputImpacts: on

Postprocess: {
  Toggle: on
  ResonantParticles: { Token: on }
  EnhancementCounter: {
    Token: on
    MinimumEC: 0.01
    SEYFileName1: copper.dat
    BoundarySurfaceID1: 6
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>The emitter box restricts launch sites to a patch of the cavity wall near the equator, which keeps the run short. The normalization line spans the 90 mm cavity length, plus a little, so that the scan levels are accelerating gradients in the usual sense. The beam pipe ends absorb.</p> <p>Run it with 16 threads on one rank; the three levels finish in about two minutes:</p> <pre><code>export OMP_NUM_THREADS=16
srun -n 1 -c 16 track3p Pillbox.track3p
</code></pre> <p>The end of <code>track3p.log</code> reports the scale factor applied to the normalized Omega3P field at each level:</p> <pre><code>number of all emitting faces = 16
scale 1.144609569160e+06 Field 2.300000000000e+07
scale 1.194375202602e+06 Field 2.400000000000e+07
scale 1.244140836043e+06 Field 2.500000000000e+07
Done!
</code></pre>

Tesla-PEmission.track3p<pre><code>ParticlesTrajectories: {
  Skip: 2                    // save every other step
}
TotalTime: 6

FieldScales: {
  Type: FieldGradient
  ScanToken: 0
  Scale: 16.00e+6            // 16 MV/m
}

NormalizedField: {
  StartPoint: 0.0 0.0 0.0
  EndPoint:   0.0 0.0 1.036200   // full 9-cell length
}

Domain: {
  FieldDir: ./omega3p_results
  Bins: 640                  // long structure: finer binning
  MaxImpacts: 2              // drop particles after two impacts
}

Emitter: {
  Type: 7                    // FieldEmission
  BoundaryID: 6
  t0: 0
  t1: 1
  x0: 0.0   x1: 0.104
  y0: 0.0   y1: 0.104
  z0: 0.0   z1: 0.114        // first cell only
  N: 1000
  WorkFunction: 4.2
  Beta: 120
}

Material: { Type: Primary    BoundarySurfaceID: 6 }
Material: { Type: Secondary  BoundarySurfaceID: 6 }
Material: { Type: Absorber   BoundarySurfaceID: 1 2 }

OutputImpacts: on
</code></pre> <p><code>Bins: 640</code> matters here. The default of 64 is tuned for a compact cavity; a one-meter structure with fine irises needs many more bins for particle localization to be efficient. <code>MaxImpacts: 2</code> keeps the run to primaries and their first bounce, since secondaries are switched off by the absence of a <code>Model: 2</code> secondary material.</p> <p>Run with the results directory named explicitly:</p> <pre><code>srun -n 16 track3p Tesla-PEmission.track3p track3p_PEmission
</code></pre>

TW7Cell.s3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: TW7Cell.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition : {
    Waveguide: 1 2         // input and output couplers
    Exterior: 6
    Magnetic: 3 4 5        // 5 is the symmetry plane
  }
}
FiniteElement: { Order: 2  CurvedSurfaces: on }
FrequencyScan: { Start: 2.8566e+9  End: 2.8566e+9  Interval: 0.0001e+9 }
PostProcess: { Toggle: on  PortNumber: 1 }
Port: { ReferenceNumber: 1  NumberOfModes: 1 }
Port: { ReferenceNumber: 2  NumberOfModes: 1 }
</code></pre> <p>A single frequency, 2.8566 GHz, with the field saved for excitation from port 1. The stored S3P result shows the structure is well matched: |S<sub>11</sub>| is 0.0045 and |S<sub>21</sub>| is 0.99999, so essentially all power travels through.</p>

TW7Cell-PEmission.track3p<pre><code>ParticlesTrajectories: { Skip: 10 }
TotalTime: 5

FieldScales: {
  Type: FieldGradient
  ScanToken: 0
  Scale: 21.6e+6
}
NormalizedField: {
  StartPoint: 0.0 0.0 -0.087275
  EndPoint:   0.0 0.0  0.087675
}

Domain: {
  FieldDir: ./s3p_results
  Bins: 128
  MaxImpacts: 2
  SymmetryFactor: 2            // half model
  SymmetryPlane: 5             // its sideset
}

Emitter: {
  Type: 7                      // Fowler-Nordheim
  BoundaryID: 6
  t0: 0  t1: 1
  x0: 0.0   x1: 0.04
  y0: -0.017  y1: 0.017
  z0: -0.02   z1: -0.01        // one iris region
  N: 100
  WorkFunction: 4.2
  Beta: 50
}

Material: { Type: Primary        BoundarySurfaceID: 6 }
Material: { Type: Secondary      BoundarySurfaceID: 6 }
Material: { Type: SymmetryPlane  BoundarySurfaceID: 5 }
Material: { Type: Absorber       BoundarySurfaceID: 1 2 3 4 }

OutputImpacts: on
Postprocess: {
  Toggle: on
  FaradayCup: { Token: on  BoundaryID: 3 4 6 }
}
</code></pre> <p>Three things differ from the Tesla case:</p> <ul> <li>Traveling-wave field. <code>FieldDir</code> points at <code>s3p_results</code>, and the gradient normalization line spans the whole structure. The field pattern is a traveling wave, so emitted electrons see a phase that depends on where they start along z.</li> <li>Symmetry plane. Sideset 5 is given <code>Material: { Type: SymmetryPlane }</code> and <code>Domain</code> declares <code>SymmetryFactor: 2</code> and <code>SymmetryPlane: 5</code>. Particles reaching the plane reflect, and the Faraday cup totals are scaled to the full structure. With <code>Absorber</code> on this plane, half the real trajectories would be lost.</li> <li>Enhancement factor 50, lower than the Tesla example's 120. Emission is restricted to one iris region by the emitter box.</li> </ul>

